-\abstract{Early nonce responses reflect the round function, output finalization, and measurement. We separate these stages in reduced ChaCha20 and Salsa20 using exact algebra and paired experiments on independent key cohorts. Both two-round cores exhibit recurring 512-bit structure; within-block rotations remove approximately 96\% and 94\% of their nonzero-frequency entropy contrasts. An independent scan confirms a Salsa20/2 nonce-word entropy gap of 0.79059 bits. This asymmetry precedes feed-forward, while modular addition increases it and introduces a predicted mixed nonce interaction. A conditional identity bounds the mean singleton response of Salsa20/2 output word 7 to flips in the second nonce word by 31/32 changed bits, with the remaining state fixed, and gives its exact local rank. A third-round update ends the sampled conditional affinity while substantial block-scale structure persists. Two frozen empirical models predict entropy-gap signs on new keys; retaining conditional positional profiles improves mean nonzero-frequency spectra at all tested resolutions, but not every entropy gap. Spectral entropy thus interprets construction-linked responses without establishing superior detector sensitivity, an attack, or full-round insecurity.}
