 \section{Introduction}
 
-Nonce diffusion measurements combine three stages: the round function, feed-forward, and aggregation of the output response. ChaCha20 and Salsa20 share addition, rotation, and exclusive OR (XOR), together termed ARX operations, and 512-bit blocks, but differ in state layout and round schedule~\citep{nir2018,bernstein2008salsa}. Reduced rounds expose their incomplete diffusion and allow these stages to be examined separately.
-
-We hold the key fixed, perturb the public initialization vector (IV), here the nonce, and measure ordered Hamming weights (HW) of output differences. This chosen-IV setting is established in cryptanalysis~\citep{englund2007,fischer2008}. Our contribution connects three levels of evidence: exact dependency and conditional-rank identities for the round function; paired interventions isolating finalization effects; and rotation, resolution, and predictive controls explaining the measured spectrum. A third-round experiment tests whether the loss of a local algebraic property coincides with the disappearance of block-scale structure. Direct diagnostics provide a benchmark throughout. Spectral entropy is an interpretive statistic of these responses, not an estimate of keystream entropy or a measure of security.
+Nonce diffusion measurements combine the round function, feed-forward, and output aggregation. ChaCha20 and Salsa20 share addition, rotation, and exclusive OR (XOR), termed ARX operations, but differ in state layout and round schedule~\citep{nir2018,bernstein2008salsa}. Reduced rounds allow these stages to be examined separately.
+
+We hold the key fixed, perturb the public initialization vector (IV), here the nonce, and measure ordered Hamming weights (HW) of output differences~\citep{englund2007,fischer2008}. Conditional identities, paired finalizations, and predictive controls connect local mechanisms to measured spectra. Spectral entropy summarizes these responses rather than keystream entropy or security; direct diagnostics provide a benchmark.
 
