 \subsection{Common Inputs, References, and Independent Cohorts}
 
-Every principal experiment uses 80 matched pairs of separately derived 256-bit keys, one key per cipher, and four shared 64-bit base nonces $v$ per pair. Salsa20 uses $v$ and ChaCha20 uses the 96-bit nonce $0^{32}\Vert v$. Counters start at zero. Separate BLAKE2b domains derive keys and control secrets~\citep{saarinen2015}; SHAKE256 derives the base nonces and computational reference streams~\citep{nist2015sha3}. At each key/cipher/configuration, a unique nonce identifies one reference stream, reused whenever that nonce recurs. Collision checks ensure disjoint base/variant groups. Under the ideal random-function model, differences sharing a base stream are independent uniform strings when their variants are distinct. SHAKE256 provides a reproducible reference with this input-reuse pattern; it is not a proof of ideal randomness.
